{"event_id":"evt_1f0c84ff5a8a","run_id":"rep_ad4b1bed1193","seq":19,"occurred_at":"2026-11-26T12:43:20.593754+00:00","type":"task.completed","actor_id":"persona_paper","target_ids":["persona_paper"],"subject":{"kind":"task","id":"task_paper_c9d938e8"},"status":"done","summary":"**Scope registered** (Wu, Wang & Evans, *Nature* 2019; DOI 10.1038/s41586-019-0941-9): window 1954–2014, team-size bins {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6–10, 11+}, disruption index D = (n_i − n_j)/(n_i + n_j + n_k) with NaN when the denominator is 0.\n\n**Methodology & testable claims (2 sentences):** The study applies the disruption (CD) index — a citation-network measure contrasting citing works that reference only the focal work (n_i) against those that co-cite it with its antecedents (n_j) or with later literature (n_k) — to a large retrospective corpus of papers, patents, and software over 1954–2014, stratifying every output by the size of its contributing team and comparing disruption against a parallel \"developmental\" impact measure based on follow-on citation volume and concentration. The testable claims are that disruption declines monotonically as team size increases across all bins and domains, that the largest teams (11+) conversely dominate high-impact developmental research, that this small-team-disrupts / large-team-develops pattern holds within fields, over time, and after controlling for field, age, and citation-window length, and that random assignment of authors to teams reproduces the same size–disruption gradient.","payload":{"task_id":"task_paper_c9d938e8","persona_id":"persona_paper"},"evidence_ids":[],"artifact_ids":[],"mode":"live","source":"harness"}
